course: dictionary
lesson: 5282
title: Juicy: more than fruit
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word juicy can mean three different things. To choose the right one, look at the words around it, which is the context.
explain: «The peach was ripe and juicy.» The word ripe tells you the peach is good to eat, so juicy means full of juice.
explain: «Everyone was talking about the juicy gossip.» Gossip is news about people. Here juicy is informal and means interesting and exciting.
explain: «Mia won a juicy prize.» A prize is something you win, so juicy here means large or rewarding.
fact: «Juicy» means full of juice, such as a ripe fruit or well-cooked meat.
fact: Informally, «juicy» means interesting and exciting, especially gossip or news.
fact: «Juicy» can also mean large or rewarding, like a juicy prize.
fact: A strawberry carries about 200 seeds on its outside, and its red juicy part is the swollen base of the flower.
fact: Sharing gossip can hurt other people, so think before you pass on a juicy story.
exercise: «In the park you spot a bush covered in shiny berries, red and juicy like sweets.» What does juicy mean here? | Very old | Lost in the park | Boring and dull | *Full of juice
exercise: Ella whispers, «Have you heard the juicy gossip about Sam?» What does juicy mean here? | Dry and dusty | *Interesting and exciting news | Full of juice
exercise: «Ben won a juicy prize for his painting.» What does juicy suggest about the prize? | *Large and rewarding | Short and rude | Dry and cracked
test:
«The steak was juicy and tender.» What does juicy mean here? | Cold and hard | *Full of juice | Very old | Lost and alone
Mia says, «The juicy story about the mayor is everywhere.» Juicy here means? | Full of juice | *Interesting and exciting | Dry and dusty | Quiet and soft
exam:
Which sentence uses juicy to mean full of juice? | Dad says the news is juicy, so we must listen | *The orange was sweet and juicy | The prize was juicy and large | Everyone loves the juicy gossip
